WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.190
Film Courage: What’s your process for developing
an idea?

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:04.620
Matthew Berkowitz, Filmmaker: That’s difficult.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:09.290
Film Courage: What are you doing before you
write the screenplay?

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:13.510
Matthew: Well I’ll come up with an idea
right?

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:18.580
Or I’ll come up with a character or I’ll
come up with a theory and most of the time

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:27.680
for me it’s like a thesis, it’s something
and I think that might be because I love to

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.070
read so much literature and I kind of come
from philosophy and I develop a thesis or

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:41.260
something that I want to say and then I find
characters that I think can embody that purpose

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:48.570
and that can function in a story that kind
of carries this idea throughout it so I can

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:53.960
communicate what I want to say in a story
and through characters.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:01:02.780
So it always starts with an idea or a thesis
or a theory that I to talk about and the characters

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.680
in the story kind of fall in line from there.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:13.970
Then I’ll just kind of binge watch a bunch
of movies that apply and I’ll just write…you

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:19.000
know I carry a little notebook around me everywhere
I go because I don’t really type.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.680
I mean I type but I don’t really write my
script until I’ve handwritten it a bunch

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:30.690
of times and a bunch of different ways and
I’ve kind of flushed out my characters and

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:37.500
only then do I write the plot because I think
that at least right now in the stories that

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.210
I’m trying to tell it’s the characters
motivate the plot rather than the plot motivating

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:43.210
the characters.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:46.210
So I want to really know the characters like
they are friends of mine.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:50.040
Like they are my best friends, my childhood
friends.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.190
So once I know them, I kind of know the world.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:59.110
Once I know the world, I know the story…so
that’s my process.

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.590
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:07.850
Do you ever get lonely when you finish writing
something because they were like your best

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:08.850
friend?

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:12.220
Because in a sense if it stops you’re almost
losing them for a little bit?

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:16.170
Matthew: I’ve never really thought about
it like that?

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:19.560
After you finish a film or finish a script
you’re kind of like “What now?”

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:20.560
You know?

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:21.620
“What do I do know?”

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:26.290
Because you’ve spent this three month period
of time every day thinking about these characters,

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:28.790
thinking about writing and thinking about
what’s next, thinking about how they say

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:30.020
this, think about how they’d say that.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:37.090
So when it’s done you’re kind of like
“Oh man, I don’t want it to be over yet.”

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:41.490
And I think it’s the same thing when you
finish a film and you’re like “Oh my gosh,

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.060
I’m done shooting…that’s it?”

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.810
It happened so quickly.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:50.250
That’s why you’ve got to get into editing
so you can keep it alive somehow and then

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:57.540
by the time you’re done you’ve got this
file that’s like a 120 gigabytes and it

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:07.090
took you a year and a half to make it and
you’re like “A year and a half turns into

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:10.620
a 120 gigabyte file or a DVD?”

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:15.989
So it’s kind of a weird sensation that you
have when you’re like all this boiled down

00:03:15.989 --> 00:03:20.550
to this little thing that you can double-click
in a place and you just put it in the DVD

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:22.020
player and it plays.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:30.800
But I think that’s what the whole process
is, it justifies the characters that you want

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:33.200
to write because they do get to play.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.709
They do get to be on a big screen, they do
get to go someplace and other people can watch

00:03:37.709 --> 00:03:40.160
and live and experience it for two hours.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:45.160
So although when you are done with the script
it’s kind of over, it’s not really over

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:48.800
because the audience gets to live through
them, your audience gets to view it and it’s

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:54.849
like the second reincarnation of your film
is like having other people be touched by

00:03:54.849 --> 00:03:55.529
what you’ve made.

